body:
@domenic,

> Chrome has a dialog implementation, and we do not support the change

Why not?

@domenic,

> That was discussed in detail in the previous threads.

I waded into them but could not find a discrete reason(s). If you have a comment link or similar I would appreciate it. I am only asking instead of wading in again because I suspect you know better than I where to find it.

@annevk wrote:

> @cookiecrook has your thinking on this perhaps changed since #4184 (comment) or do you still agree with that? As I understand it Firefox's accessibility team still thinks this is the way to go.

No change in opinion. If no autofocus specified, focusing the dialog should be the default.

This opinion is based on loads of discussion with browser implementors, screen reader developers, web authors, and some web-savvy screen reader users. Several of those making comments above in favor of this PR can claim 3 or more of those titles. 

I take no issue with @domenic's proposal in #2197 to allowing opt-out of the focus behavior, but the default behavior should be to focus the dialog if no focus information (or opt-out) is provided by the author.

@domenic wrote:
> In general we shouldn't rely on year-old affirmations from people who may or may not still be working on the relevant area of code; if you're trying to land a change now, it's good idea to get interest now.

@nt1m is working on this now and can add more.
